08", "marc:CollingswoodLibraryMarcDump10-27-2008/Collingswood.out:13720620:2309", "ia:noordinarytimefr00good", "ia:noordinarytimef000good", "bwb:9780684804484", "marc:harvard_bibliographic_metadata/20220215_033.bib.mrc:54775349:5545"], "title": "No ordinary time", "notes": {"type": "/type/text", "value": "First published: 1994.\n\n\"A Touchstone book.\"\n\nIncludes bibliographical references (p. [715]-725) and index."}, "identifiers": {"goodreads": ["18728"], "librarything": ["13591"]}, "languages": [{"key": "/languages/eng"}], "subjects": ["Roosevelt, Franklin D. -- 1882-1945", "Roosevelt, Eleanor, -- 1884-1962", "World War, 1939-1945 -- United States", "Presidents -- United States -- Biography", "Presidents' spouses -- United States -- Biography", "United States -- History -- 1933-1945"], "publish_date": "1995", "publish_country": "nyu", "by_statement": "Doris Kearns Goodwin.", "oclc_numbers": ["33319965"], "works": [{"key": "/works/OL1856005W"}], "type": {"key": "/type/edition"}, "lc_classifications": ["E807.G66 1994", "E807 .G66 1994"], "description": {"type": "/type/text", "value": "Presenting an aspect of American history that has never been fully told, this Pulitzer Prize-winning work paints a detailed, intimate portrait of FDR and Eleanor Roosevelt and provides a brilliant narrative account of America during wartime. Photos. No Ordinary Time is a monumental work, a brilliantly conceived chronicle of one of the most vibrant and revolutionary periods in the history of the United States. With an extraordinary collection of details, Goodwin masterfully weaves together a striking number of story lines--Eleanor and Franklin's marriage and remarkable partnership, Eleanor's life as First Lady, and FDR's White House and its impact on America as well as on a world at war. Goodwin effectively melds these details and stories into an unforgettable and intimate portrait of Eleanor and Franklin Roosevelt and of the time during which a new, modern America was born."}, "latest_revision": 13, "revision": 13, "created": {"type": "/type/datetime", "value": "2008-09-12T15:15:22.047247"}, "last_modified": {"type": "/type/datetime", "value": "2026-03-18T18:49:54.023664"}}}, "ISBN:0593189353": {"bib_key": "ISBN:0593189353", "info_url": "http://openlibrary.org/books/OL30192431M/Once_a_Warrior", "preview": "noview", "preview_url": "http://openlibrary.org/books/OL30192431M/Once_a_Warrior", "thumbnail_url": "https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/10539361-S.jpg", "details": {"publishers": ["Penguin Publishing Group"], "subtitle": "How One Veteran Found a New Mission Closer to Home", "source_records": ["bwb:9780593189351", "promise:bwb_daily_pallets_2022-12-14"], "title": "Once a Warrior", "number_of_pages": 320, "isbn_13": ["9780593189351"], "languages": [{"key": "/languages/eng"}], "full_title": "Once a Warrior How One Veteran Found a New Mission Closer to Home", "lc_classifications": ["", "HV553.W647 2020"], "publish_date": "2020", "key": "/books/OL30192431M", "authors": [{"key": "/authors/OL8474903A", "name": "Jake Wood"}], "works": [{"key": "/works/OL22158728W"}], "type": {"key": "/type/edition"}, "covers": [10539361], "local_id": ["urn:bwbsku:W8-AOC-152"], "latest_revision": 4, "revision": 4, "created": {"type": "/type/datetime", "value": "2020-09-16T10:38:53.701917"}, "last_modified": {"type": "/type/datetime", "value": "2022-12-22T09:09:16.332044"}}}, "ISBN:0307409600": {"bib_key": "ISBN:0307409600", "info_url": "http://openlibrary.org/books/OL26692646M/Presidents_of_War", "preview": "restricted", "preview_url": "https://archive.org/details/presidentsofwar00mich", "thumbnail_url": "https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/15216174-S.jpg", "details": {"table_of_contents": [{"level": 0, "title": "\"Torrent of passion\"", "type": {"key": "/type/toc_item"}}, {"level": 0, "title": "\"Man of straw\"", "type": {"key": "/type/toc_item"}}, {"level": 0, "title": "\"The most glorious war\"", "type": {"key": "/type/toc_item"}}, {"level": 0, "title": "\"The country is now virtually ours\"", "type": {"key": "/type/toc_item"}}, {"level": 0, "title": "\"A presidential war\"", "type": {"key": "/type/toc_item"}}, {"level": 0, "title": "Fort Sumter", "type": {"key": "/type/toc_item"}}, {"level": 0, "title": "\"Blood flowing all about me\"", "type": {"key": "/type/toc_item"}}, {"level": 0, "title": "\"Maine blown up\"", "type": {"key": "/type/toc_item"}}, {"level": 0, "title": "\"We must keep what we want\"", "type": {"key": "/type/toc_item"}}, {"level": 0, "title": "\"The world is on fire\"", "type": {"key": "/type/toc_item"}}, {"level": 0, "title": "\"Salvation of mankind\"", "type": {"key": "/type/toc_item"}}, {"level": 0, "title": "\"How could this thing happen?\"", "type": {"key": "/type/toc_item"}}, {"level": 0, "title": "\"The survival war\"", "type": {"key": "/type/toc_item"}}, {"level": 0, "title": "\"I am going to let them have it\"", "type": {"key": "/type/toc_item"}}, {"level": 0, "title": "\"I don't ask their permission\"", "type": {"key": "/type/toc_item"}}, {"level": 0, "title": "\"We got slapped\"", "type": {"key": "/type/toc_item"}}, {"level": 0, "title": "\"I do not have a parachute.\"", "type": {"key": "/type/toc_item"}}], "description": {"type": "/type/text", "value": "Presidential historian Michael Beschloss uses original letters, diaries, declassified documents, and interviews to bring us into the room and into the minds of a procession of Chief Executives who took the nation into major conflicts, mobilized Americans for victory, and seized greater power for themselves. From James Madison and the War of 1812 to Lyndon Johnson and Vietnam, we see these leaders struggling with Congress, the courts, the press, their own advisers, and antiwar protesters; seeking comfort from their spouses, families, and friends; and dropping to their knees in prayer. We come to understand how these Presidents were able to withstand the pressures of war -- both physically and emotionally -- or were broken by them."}, "isbn_10":